<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fife</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kingdom of Fife</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fife is a council area and historic county of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, a peninsula between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-tay">Firth of Tay</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>. It takes its name from the Pictish province of Fib and was held by the powerful earls of Fife in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Its towns include Dunfermline, burial place of Scottish kings, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a>, the country's ecclesiastical capital and home of its oldest university.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fife.jpg" alt="Fife" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kim Traynor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crail,_Fife,_Scotland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
